Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public.
Dispute 21 - Day 18

Mastering Round 1  

Bruce did you think I forgot about you? Well Absolutely Not!!
You're too Awesomely Amazing to forget. I was just giving you time to
get your life together and correct those Personal Information letters!
By now, you should have updated the personal information. The next step
is to send a dispute letter to the Credit Reporting Agencies (CRA) 

**Section 611 (5)(A**) of the FCRA - you are required to 

**"...promptly DELETE all information which cannot be verified."**In
short, if the credit bureaus cannot verify the account or inquiry is
yours in a timely matter (30-45 business days) the account must be
removed off of your report. If this is the first time you are disputing
an item, or multiple items, on a credit report, we call that a Round 1
letter.

Before beginning Round 1, you want to carefully analyze the most
recently pulled credit report. When analyzing the report, it's
important to determine which accounts are negatively impacting the
credit score the most. Generally, these are the accounts that have
become negative or delinquent within the past 24 months. You will want
to focus on those. It is also important to note that there may be other
negative accounts on the credit report that you will include that are
beyond the 24 month mark and there are other negative accounts that you
will want to leave alone. More on this below... 

The Round 1 letter is always sent to the credit reporting agency (CRA).
The CRA that you begin Round 1 with will depend on the type of account
you are disputing and your dispute strategy or action plan. You can
refer back to a previous lesson that discusses various CRAs aside from
the Big 3. 

In the Round 1 letter you are asking the CRA to Re-Investigate the
account(s) listed in your letter and verify whether the account
information for each is reporting with maximum accuracy. It's
pertinent that when you are writing your dispute reason you are very
clear and concise. Most letters go unanswered or do not have the desired
action taken because there is a lack of clarity in what is being asked
of the CRA. The absolute must in a dispute letter is that a reasonable
person must be able to tell that you are disputing an item. Simply
stating that you've recently looked at your credit report and found
inaccurate information then listing the Account Name and Account Number
is not enough detail to determine why you are disputing an item and what
action you would like taken at the end of the investigation. I like to
break down the dispute reason into 3 steps, called the Dispute Reason
Formula. Your dispute reason should contain the following: a Reason, a
Request and a Result. 

·        State your "

**Reason**" for disputing the account: Inaccurate Information

·        State your "

**Request**": Re-investigate and Verify all data for this account is
reporting accurate including every notation, dates and balances, whether
reported or not

·        State your desired "

**Result**": immediately delete this account from my report/ correct
this account to report as...(based on what you would like to happen with
the account).

Simply asking the credit bureaus to just "Validate or verify account" or
"Unverified Account" is not enough. The credit bureaus get the same
responses every single day.

If you remember from the dispute letter writing lesson, you want to
ensure that your letter sounds as if it's coming from a consumer.
Think "least sophisticated consumer." This is especially important for
Round 1 letters so that your disputes aren't immediately blocked,
causing your chances of getting the account(s) deleted to diminish. 

The 30-Day Time Limit - The FCRA imposes a thirty-day time limit on the
dispute. If the item is not responded to within that time limit, it must
be removed from your credit file. Using their automated dispute system
makes it very easy for the credit reporting bureaus and reporting
agencies to not only respond, but to respond without having to do a full
investigation. This is why you want to ensure that your letters are
basic but customized and your dispute reason is clear.

Within 30 - 45 days of mailing off your letter(s) you are likely to
receive one of the following responses to your dispute. 

·       

**The item was deleted / corrected:** Horary!!! Mission Accomplished.
Now just keep an eye on the proceeding reports to ensure the item
doesn't mysteriously pop back up or those corrections are don't
revert back to inaccurate reporting on the next update.

·       

**This item has been verified:** This most likely means that the bureau
or reporting agency checked against their own flawed system and spat out
this response. They most likely didn't really investigate anything at
this early stage. 

**Next Step:** Proceed to Round 2 and challenge the bureau by asking
them to "prove it" by telling you what creditor they contacted and how
they verified it. You can even try by asking them to provide you with a
document bearing your signature, but most likely that won't happen and
they are not required to do so. You might also choose to send a letter
to the creditor or collection agency by asking them to provide proof or
debt validation by way of a document with a signature proving that the
debt is yours and was agreed to.

****
·       

**Suspicious Letter or Not Enough Information:**This could simply be a
stall tactic used to buy themselves more time or sent in hope that the
consumer does not know the proper steps to take next, gets frustrated
and gives up. Or, it could mean that you forgot to include the
client's photo ID and proof of address. 

**Next Step:** Resend the same letter as before, inserting a handwritten
note or a line at the beginning of the letter that says "Oops I forgot
to include my Photo ID and Proof of Address, here you go" and be sure to
include the identification documentation. (Note: Personal Information
Letters and Round 1 letters are the only letters that require
Identification Documents.)

****
·       

**Your dispute is frivolous:**Frivolous means to not have any serious
purpose or value. You may receive this response when you lack clarity in
your reason for disputing or if the account has been previously disputed
using the same reason. 

**Next Step:** (1) Resend the Round 1 letter clarifying your dispute
reason, using the 3 R Dispute Formula. (2) Respond with a letter
requesting a reason for why they believe this to be the case and ask
what you need to do to have the matter investigated, again including
your disputed accounts.

****
·       

**We suspect that you are working with a Credit Repair Company: This
result is most common when**too much legalese is used, especially in
Round 1, or it doesn't seem like the Investigation letter is being sent
by a consumer. 

**Next Step:** Remove the legal jargon, if any. Re-send Round One Letter
responding to their accusation and ensure them that you've done your
research and you know your rights.

·       

**Your letter is not legible:**This response is either a stall tactic or
it means that your letter was too difficult to understand.

**Next Step:**Go back to the original letter you sent. Check to make
sure that your dispute reasons are written clearly and makes sense. Wait
approximately 5 - 7 days to see if you receive another response from
the bureau. If so, they were just stalling to by themselves more time.
If not, resend the same letter - dispute reasons written clearly -
with a handwritten note on the original letter explaining that your
letter is indeed legible and demand that they investigate per your
request.

Why All Negative Account Should Not Be Deleted

Some derogatory accounts can actually raise your credit score, or having
them deleted can actually lower your score more than just leaving them
reporting. For example, if you had a $5,000 charged-off account - paid
in full - the balance, paid status and payment history could account in
the scoring of your file. That's why deleting some derogatory accounts
can actually lower your credit score! So be careful when deciding which
accounts you want to have deleted. Some you may just want to have
correct and others you may want to leave alone altogether. 

